    "reconstruction": {
      "decoder": "fresh-source-blind-statement-decoder-37",
      "reconstructed_theorem_text": "For every function f : R -> R that is twice continuously differentiable and has compact support, define, for each natural number m, the Boolean cube B_m = {epsilon : Fin m -> Bool}, the probability measure mu_m = |B_m|^{-1} times counting measure, and the real-valued normalized sign sum S_m(epsilon) = (sqrt(m))^{-1} sum_{j in Fin m} sigma(epsilon_j), where sigma(true) = 1 and sigma(false) = -1. The inverse of zero is zero, so S_0 is zero and the sum over Fin 0 is empty. Let gamma be the actual real Gaussian probability measure with mean zero and variance one. For each j in Fin m, let epsilon^(j) be epsilon with only its j-th Boolean coordinate negated, and define E_m(epsilon) = sum_{j in Fin m} (f(S_m(epsilon^(j))) - f(S_m(epsilon)))^2, with no factor of one half.\nThen all three conclusions hold: (1) x |-> (f'(x))^2 is Bochner integrable with respect to gamma; (2) for every natural number m, including m = 0, E_m is Bochner integrable with respect to mu_m; and (3) as n tends to infinity through the natural numbers, the real Bochner integral of E_{n+1} with respect to mu_{n+1} converges to 4 times the real Bochner integral of (f')^2 with respect to gamma. The only caller assumptions are C2 regularity and compact support of f; integrability and convergence are conclusions.",
      "reconstructed_text_sha256": "f030995bb3fc843e71fa4c7a0918d97f8c0f0b1ec12962f4390838143e0d4a63",
